 43. Never end a turn while the loop has work unless something will wake you. During `building` or `changing`, a turn may end only when (a) a background agent, or a background command **of a kind that finishes**, is still running — its completion notification wakes you: a builder run, a Review B run, the CI watch, a test suite you await, run through the launcher (`--limit auto --kind test`), or a mutation run or another driver, run through it too (`--kind driver`), or a benchmark, or a test runner the launcher does not know by name (it answers exit 125), run directly in the background, each marked with the comment `# wake` (a wrapper of the project's own that itself calls the launcher is marked `# wake builder` or `# wake review`, and the guard gives it that kind's ceiling; 2026.10.11c); an unknown runner also gets an Issues entry naming it, so the launcher learns it. A background command of yours is ended with `python ~/.claude/cursor-bridge/bridge-stop.py <run id>` (a launcher run, from its pid file) or `--pid <pid>`, which ends its whole process tree after checking that the pid is still the launcher's (a loop not started through the launcher: its pid from `tasklist /V /FI "IMAGENAME eq bash.exe"`, then `--pid`); `TaskStop` ends the loop and not its children, which is how a stopped verification loop advanced into its next driver (KP-056). A reviewer that ends at its turn limit (`maxTurns`) without a verdict is no verdict: run it once more on the part of the diff it had not reached, then record an issue. A server, a worker or a probe you started is never a wake source: it never finishes, so nothing would wake you (KP-031). A background agent counts only under its ceiling, an hour unless `Agent ceiling:` in the run parameters says more (release A2, plan 10.3): when you end a turn to wait for one, write it on the status file's `In flight:` line (or, while a review runs on a snapshot, in `run/supervisor/in-flight.md`, copied to the status file after `verify`, so the reviewed tree is not changed) and start its ceiling timer in the background: `python ~/.claude/cursor-bridge/wake-timer.py <seconds> "<the agent>"` (copy the command the loop guard's refusal prints, with the Bash tool's `timeout` at its seconds plus a minute, in milliseconds; the timer's completion wakes you at the ceiling), because a hung agent never completes, and a scheduled wake-up (`CronCreate`) does not fire while a background agent runs, since the session is not idle for the scheduler (KP-043, proven 2026-10-11; KP-039 for the clock arithmetic of any wake-up you still schedule for an idle wait); at that wake-up or at any checkpoint, stop an agent older than its ceiling with `TaskStop` and run its task again in the foreground, where the reviewer's turn limit bounds it; the loop guard refuses a stop that waits on an agent with no timer running to its ceiling — or (b) you are waiting for the owner, and the `Awaiting user on:` line of `PROJECT_STATUS.md` says so: an escalation, a gate, a pause the owner asked for, a stage pause. Waiting for CI is (a): one background `gh pr checks <n> --watch --required --fail-fast`; the desktop app's CI monitor never reports a green gate. Clear `Awaiting user on:` to `nothing` the moment the owner answers. The `loop-guard` Stop hook enforces this: when the status file says the loop is running, nobody is being waited on, and nothing is pending, it refuses the stop once with the reason; it never blocks twice in a row, outside a bridge Workspace, or in any other phase. It reads the background tasks and scheduled wake-ups Claude Code hands it, and the session record only when that list is absent.
